    Properties: Read, Write, Setting
    Type: Integer (64 bit)
    """
    auto: MFLIImpsAuto
    bias: MFLIImpsBias
    calib: MFLIImpsCalib
    confidence: MFLIImpsConfidence
    current: MFLIImpsCurrent
    demod: MFLIImpsDemod
    discard: MFLIImpsDiscard
    enable: ZIParameter[int, int]
    """Enable impedance calculation for demodulator data.

    Node: /DEV..../IMPS/n/ENABLE
    Properties: Read, Write, Setting
    Type: Integer (64 bit)
    """
    freq: ZIParameter[float, float]
    """Frequency control for the oscillator used for impedance measurement.

    Node: /DEV..../IMPS/n/FREQ
    Properties: Read, Write, Setting
    Type: Double
    Unit: Hz
    """
    interpolation: ZIParameter[int, int | str]
    """Select the interpolation method of the compensation data. The interpolation
    method is particularly important if the derivative changes strongly e.g at cut-
    off frequencies.

    Node: /DEV..../IMPS/n/INTERPOLATION
    Properties: Read, Write, Setting
    Type: Integer (enumerated)

    Options:
        0: "linear": Linear: The linear interpolation is fastest but may create
            compensation errors in between the frequency points used for
            compensation.
        1: "pchip": Piecewise Cubic Hermite (PCHIP): The piecewise cubic Hermite
            interpolation will result in very accurate results but requires more
            calculation power.
    """
    maxbandwidth: ZIParameter[float, float]
    """Limit of the maximum bandwidth used on the demodulator filter. Values above 1 kHz
    can heavily diminish measurement accuracy in the high-frequency region where the
    amplitude is no more constant over frequency.

    Node: /DEV..../IMPS/n/MAXBANDWIDTH
    Properties: Read, Write, Setting
    Type: Double
    Unit: Hz
    """
    mode: ZIParameter[int, int | str]
    """Select impedance measurement mode.

    Node: /DEV..../IMPS/n/MODE
    Properties: Read, Write, Setting
    Type: Integer (enumerated)

    Options:
        0: 4 Terminal: This method uses the current and the voltage drop across the
            DUT to calculate the DUT impedance. This method results in very accurate
            measurements as influences of series resistors on the output and current
            input are excluded.
        1: 2 Terminal: This method uses the driving voltage and the measured current
            to calculate the DUT impedance. The 2 Terminal method can be beneficial
            when measuring very high impedance where the parasitics of the voltage
            measurement are limiting the frequency range.
    """
    model: ZIParameter[int, int | str]
    """Representation of the complex impedance value Z by two real values accessible as
    Parameter 1 and Parameter 2 on all user interface displays.

    Node: /DEV..../IMPS/n/MODEL
    Properties: Read, Write, Setting
    Type: Integer (enumerated)

    Options:
        0: "r_c_parallel": Rp || Cp: Impedance value Z is represented by a resistive
            element Rp in parallel with a capacitive element Cp.
        1: "r_c_series": Rs + Cs: Impedance value Z is represented by a resistive
            element Rs in series with a capacitive element Cs.
        2: "r_l_series": Rs + Ls: Impedance value Z is represented by a resistive
            element Rs in series with an inductive element Ls.
        3: "g_b_parallel", "admittance": G B: Impedance value Z is represented by
            conductance G = Real(Y) and Susceptance B = Imag(Y) of the admittance Y =
            1/Z.
        4: "d_c_series": D Cs: Impedance value Z is represented by a dissipation
            factor D = -Real(Z)/Imag(Z) (loss tangent) and a series capacitive
            element.
        5: "q_c_series": Q Cs: Impedance value Z is represented by a quality factor Q
            = -Imag(Z)/Real(Z) and a series capacitive element.
        6: "d_l_series": D Ls: Impedance value Z is represented by a dissipation
            factor D = Real(Z)/Imag(Z) (loss tangent) and a series inductive element.
        7: "q_l_series": Q Ls: Impedance value Z is represented by a quality factor Q
            = Imag(Z)/Real(Z) and a series inductive element.
        8: "r_l_parallel": Rp || Lp: Impedance value Z is represented by a resistive
            element Rp in parallel with an inductive element Lp.
        9: "d_c_parallel": D Cp: Impedance value Z is represented by a dissipation
            factor D = Real(Z)/Imag(Z) (loss tangent) and a parallel capacitive
            element Cp.
        10: "dielectric": Dielectic: Impedance value Z is represented by an
            equivalent dieletric material with a complex permittivity.
    """
    mut: MFLIImpsMut
    omegasuppression: ZIParameter[float, float]
    """Suppression of the omega and 2-omega components. Small omega suppression can
    diminish measurements of very low or high impedance because the DC component can
    become dominant. Large omega suppression will have a significant impact on sweep
    time especially for low filter orders.

    Node: /DEV..../IMPS/n/OMEGASUPPRESSION
    Properties: Read, Write, Setting
    Type: Double
    Unit: dB
    """
    oneperiod: MFLIImpsOneperiod
    output: MFLIImpsOutput
    sample: ZIParameter[Any, Never]
    """Streaming node containing the impedance measurement sample data.

    Node: /DEV..../IMPS/n/SAMPLE
    Properties: Read, Stream
    Type: ZIImpedanceSample
    """
    voltage: MFLIImpsVoltage

